WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.620
In a newborn mouse brain, some connections will be lost.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.220
Which ones, and what carries them away?

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.250
Start in the visual thalamus, the relay where axons from the eye end.

4
00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:18.683
In a newborn mouse, inputs from the two eyes overlap there.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:24.133
Over roughly the first week and a half, the two eyes' inputs sort into separate

6
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:25.133
territories.

7
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:29.153
Each side ends up mostly the territory of the opposite eye.

8
00:00:30.013 --> 00:00:32.033
The eye on the same side keeps a small patch.

9
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.693
One relay neuron starts with many retinal inputs, and ends with only a few.

10
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:40.413
Inputs that lose are removed.

11
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:42.853
So, which inputs lose?

12
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:44.793
And what carries them away?

13
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:49.683
The sorting is driven partly by spontaneous waves of activity.

14
00:00:50.563 --> 00:00:52.923
They sweep the young retina before the eyes open.

15
00:00:53.917 --> 00:01:00.157
Predict. If activity biases the outcome, which inputs should lose more often: the

16
00:01:00.157 --> 00:01:01.977
busier ones, or the quieter ones?

17
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.853
In this circuit, experiments that change one eye's activity point to the quieter

18
00:01:12.853 --> 00:01:18.393
ones. Later, an experiment that silences one eye tests whether microglia follow.

19
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:27.887
For the second question, the obvious suspects are microglia, the brain's resident

20
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.967
immune cells and its main phagocytes.

21
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.227
Here is the one idea to carry through.

22
00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:39.327
Relative activity biases which retinal inputs lose, and microglia take relatively

23
00:01:39.327 --> 00:01:40.687
more from the quieter eye.

24
00:01:41.647 --> 00:01:43.347
Complement helps them eat part of that.

25
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:48.930
The rule has a scope.

26
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.730
This is the mouse visual thalamus, in the first week and a half after birth.

27
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.070
Complement is needed for normal sorting.

28
00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.190
These studies did not show that it carries the activity information.

29
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.660
And part means part.

30
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.100
Some eating goes on without complement's receptor.

31
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.520
Astrocytes eat synapses too.

32
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.960
So keep the two findings side by side.

33
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.300
How activity and complement connect is still open.
